 "sheet": [
  "Write as a working Romanian newspaper or magazine journalist (Adevărul, HotNews, G4Media, Forbes România), not as a translator: re-order and re-cut English sentences, keep the facts and drop the syntax.",
  "Open news with the fact in the perfect compound tense, subject first: “Guvernul a aprobat marți …”. Put the source in the same sentence or the next (“a anunțat ministerul”, “potrivit datelor Departamentului de Cadastru”), never in a throat-clearing lead.",
  "Name the who, what, how much and when inside the first two sentences: a figure, a date, a named institution or person. A paragraph with none of them gets one or is cut.",
  "Use plain Romanian verbs where AI uses copulas and nominalisations: “este”, “are”, “a deschis”, “a cumpărat”; not “reprezintă”, “constituie”, “dispune de”, “a efectuat o achiziție”, “se realizează”.",
  "Prefer active voice and finite verbs to “se + passive”, “a fost efectuată”, “urmează a fi”, “de către”, “în cadrul”, “în vederea”, “prin intermediul”, “la nivelul”, “în ceea ce privește”, “datorită faptului că”.",
  "Rotate attribution verbs and match them to the speech act: a declarat, a spus, a explicat, a precizat, a adăugat, a notat, a admis, a recunoscut, a insistat, a avertizat, a amintit, a transmis (statements), a susținut (contested claims). Never repeat the same verb in consecutive attributions.",
  "Attribute to a named person, institution or document with a role and a date. Replace “experții spun”, “studiile arată”, “se spune că” with the actual source or cut the claim.",
  "Vary sentence length on purpose: a six-word sentence next to a thirty-word one with a subordinate clause. Never three sentences in a row that open with the same word, and never three “Acesta/Aceasta” in a row.",
  "Drop redundant subject pronouns and demonstratives: Romanian carries the subject in the verb. Repeat the noun instead of chaining “acesta, aceasta, aceștia”.",
  "Do not start paragraphs with connectives. Cut “De asemenea”, “În plus”, “Mai mult”, “Totodată”, “Cu toate acestea”, “Prin urmare”, “Astfel”; let the logic sit in the sentence order, or use “Însă”, “Dar”, “Iar” inside the sentence.",
  "No gerund tails that comment on the previous clause (“…, subliniind importanța…”, “…, asigurând…”, “…, evidențiind…”). Finish the sentence and start a new one with a fact; use a gerund only for a real simultaneous action.",
  "No “nu doar … ci și”, “nu numai … ci”, “nu se limitează la”, “mai mult decât un simplu”. State what the thing is, then what else it does, in two plain clauses.",
  "Avoid tricolons by habit (“eleganță, confort și autenticitate”). Give two items or four, or one precise item; an abstract-noun triple is a machine signature.",
  "Cut significance inflation: “joacă un rol crucial”, “subliniază importanța”, “este o mărturie a”, “marchează o cotitură”. Say what changed, for whom and by how much.",
  "Never close with a moral, a summary or a forecast (“În concluzie”, “Pe scurt”, “Rămâne de văzut”, “Viitorul pare luminos”). End on the last concrete fact, the next date or deadline, a quote, or a sharp detail.",
  "Do not open with scene-setting or throat-clearing (“Este important de menționat”, “Într-o lume în continuă schimbare”, “Situat în inima Mediteranei”, “Fie că ești … fie că …”, “Iată ce trebuie să știți”). Start with the news or the image.",
  "Cyprus clichés are banned: insula Afroditei, ape cristaline, perla Mediteranei, 300 de zile de soare, destinație ideală, locație strategică, răscruce de trei continente, ospitalitate legendară. Replace each with a place name, a number or an observed detail.",
  "Kill English calques: “la sfârșitul zilei”, “o bijuterie ascunsă”, “o experiență de neuitat”, “un amestec de tradiție și modernitate”, “schimbă regulile jocului”, “unul dintre cele mai …”, “în timp ce” for every “while”. Rewrite the idea the way a Romanian would say it.",
  "Keep hype adjectives out (pitoresc, vibrant, fascinant, captivant, spectaculos, rafinat, autentic, unic, remarcabil). One concrete detail (material, price, year, name) replaces three adjectives.",
  "Estate-agent and press-release language is forbidden in editorial copy: “proprietate de lux”, “oportunitate unică”, “finisaje premium”, “soluții inovatoare”, “lider de piață”, “valoare adăugată”, “contactați-ne”. Describe the object and the deal in measurable terms.",
  "Use only comma-below ș and ț (never cedilla ş ţ), and ă, â, î correctly: î at the start and end of a word, â inside (cuvânt, sunt, într-o); never the pre-1993 spellings sînt, cînd, romîn. Never type Romanian without diacritics.",
  "Quotes are „…” (low-9 opening U+201E, closing U+201D), with «…» for a quote inside a quote. Never \"…\" straight or English “…”. Place the full stop after the closing mark unless the quote is a full sentence.",
  "No em or en dashes and no spaced hyphens as dashes. Use a comma, colon, full stop, parentheses or restructure; keep the hyphen only inside compounds and clitics (într-o, s-a, ex-ministru, sud-est).",
  "Numbers: dot as thousands separator and comma as decimal (1.200; 12,5%; 3,8 milioane). Years stay unseparated (2026). Spell out one to nine in running prose, figures from 10 up; always figures for money, percentages, areas and dates.",
  "Currency goes after the number, and amounts from 20 up take “de”: 1.200 de euro, 250 de lei, 45 de euro; but 2 euro, 15 euro, 105 euro. Do not write €1.200, EUR 1200 or 1,200 euro. The euro is invariable (două milioane de euro).",
  "Dates and times: “12 octombrie 2026”, “joi, 9 octombrie”, months and weekdays in lowercase, “ora 18.30” or “18:30”; never “October 12”, “12th”, “6 PM”, or slash dates. Areas as “120 m²” (non-breaking space) or “120 de metri pătrați”.",
  "Headings and titles use sentence case: capital only on the first word and proper nouns (“Cum se cumpără o proprietate în Limassol”), never English Title Case; no trailing full stop; no generic labels (Introducere, Concluzii, Aspecte cheie, Întrebări frecvente).",
  "Use Romanian exonyms and standard institution names: Cipru, Nicosia, Larnaca, Limassol, Paphos, Famagusta, Camera Reprezentanților, Banca Centrală a Ciprului, Departamentul de Cadastru și Topografie. Gloss a Greek or English acronym once, then use it.",
  "Keep one register per piece: third person, no “tu” and no “dumneavoastră” address in news, business and legal copy. Direct “dumneavoastră” is acceptable only in an interview question or a relocation checklist, and never mixed with tu-forms.",
  "Commas: none before “și” in a simple list, no comma between subject and predicate, a comma before dar, însă, iar, ci and after a fronted subordinate clause. Avoid piling “al/a/ai/ale” genitive chains; break them with a verb.",
  "Replace vague quantifiers (“o creștere semnificativă”, “interes crescut”, “cerere în creștere”) with the figure and its base: “+6,4% față de 2025”, “1.240 de tranzacții în primele opt luni”.",
  "Use anglicisms only when the Romanian press itself does (cash flow, hub, startup); translate the rest (“must-have”, “boutique”, “rooftop”, “insight”). Never leave a stray English function word in the text.",
  "Keep legal and tax disclaimers to one plain sentence at the end of the piece, never repeated; cite the instrument once by name, number and year, then explain its effect in one sentence a lay reader can use.",
  "Never output assistant language: no “Sper că v-a ajutat”, “Iată versiunea”, “Spune-mi dacă vrei modificări”, no placeholders, no markdown asterisks, hashes or backticks in body text."
 ],
