 "desks": {
  "news": "Lede in the perfect compound tense with the actor first, source in the same sentence or the next, figures and a date in the first two lines. Short paragraphs, no adjectives of praise, no closing moral. Headlines in present tense, sentence case, no full stop.",
  "business": "Forbes România and G4Media style: number, base and comparison first (+8,2% față de 2025), then the cause named by a person or document. Verbs of action over abstract nouns; no “dinamic”, “robust”, “sinergie”. End on the next report date or decision.",
  "property_legal": "Concrete and procedural: who signs what, at which office, within how many days, at what cost in euro. Cite the act once with number and year and say what it changes in plain words. No estate-agent adjectives, one single disclaimer sentence at most.",
  "relocation_guide": "Second-order service journalism in the third person or an impersonal imperative: steps with durations, fees in „x de euro”, the office and its name, the document list. State the catch or the delay a Romanian will actually hit. No “tot ce trebuie să știți”.",
  "economy": "Dry, precise, sourced: institution, indicator, period, comparison. Explain the mechanism in one sentence a lay reader can repeat. Avoid “peisaj economic”, “ecosistem”, “catalizator”; do not forecast beyond what the source says.",
  "culture": "Observation before opinion: name the work, the venue, the date, the people. Opinion rests on one specific detail, not on “fascinant” or “captivant”. Quotes from artists or curators, attributed with varied verbs. Close on a detail or a date, not a verdict.",
  "food": "Dish, ingredient, price, place, named cook; sensory words only when tied to a thing (crustă, fum, lămâie), never “explozie de arome” or “festin”. Cypriot terms (halloumi, souvla, meze, koupepia) kept with a short gloss at first use.",
  "fashion_lifestyle": "Vogue România register: precise nouns (croială, material, atelier, colecție), no “glamour”, “must-have”, “trendy”. Name the designer, the house and the price where known. Wit is welcome; brochure superlatives are not.",
  "travel": "One scene, one route, one price: road, time, ticket, season. Use what the writer saw, heard and paid, with place names in Romanian forms. No “bijuterie ascunsă”, “ape cristaline”, “de neuitat”; the sea is described by colour and temperature, not by cliché.",
  "events": "Service first: what, where, date and time (ora 19.30), price in euro, how to book, who performs. One sentence of context, then logistics; no “nu ratați”, “vă așteptăm”. Past events get a short report with one quote and a headcount.",
  "interview": "Short human lede (who, where, what struck you), then Q and A or a profile with direct quotes in „…” and rotated attribution verbs. Keep the interviewee’s register; address them consistently (dumneavoastră or tu, never mixed). Cut the “Mulțumim pentru interviu” closer.",
  "review": "A verdict backed by specifics: what was tried, what it cost, what failed. Balanced and sceptical, with concrete praise and concrete criticism; no “merită vizitat” without a reason. Give the price, the date visited and a practical caveat.",
  "people": "Portrait through a scene and facts: age, origin, path, the decision that changed things, with dates and figures. Quote them directly; avoid hagiography (“un om pasionat”, “o personalitate remarcabilă”). Let a detail, not an adjective, characterise."
 }
